That's a nice clean insight -- I "got religion" at the age of 4
(I still appreciate a nice sanctuary with pipe organ and such,
like at a wedding I went to recently) because of the awed feeling
("awe-robics" -- Lily Tomlin as Trudy) and gave it up at 13 or 14
when I realized that listening to Bach's Tocatta and Fugue in D
minor for 10 seconds was more hair-raising than 10 years of Methodist
services and Sunday School, even counting the rushes I got from watching
female classmates grow up and dress up and sometimes forget to "sit like 
a lady" :-)


>No, you aren't the only one like this.  Music and art have similar effects on
>me.  Feels good!

The first time I heard Guillaume de Machaut I was maybe 22 and thought
I knew everything -- I didn't bother to dry my tears until the record
was over. Since then I have sought out early music concerts and 
recordings with great zeal. My ideal party would be a fine dinner
for 8 couples in one of the small halls at the MFA in Boston,
with deadly serious wines, and music by Ensemble P.A.N.
(Project Ars Nova).

[Side note -- I am about 50 pages into "Genius" and I notice that
Gleick's publishers actually allowed him to quote an occasional
equation, despite the usual pop.sci. rule that each equation cuts
your sales in half.]



 
