From: szabo@netcom.com (Nick Szabo)
Date: Fri, 23 Sep 1994 23:14:40 -0700 (PDT)
Subject: [#94-9-444] SCI: role for Q.M in neurobiology

Paul Cisek: 
> Another important question: are these effects observed in cells taken from
> diverse taxonomic branches?

And a third question: how can we manipulate and improve upon these
biological quantum coherence mechanisms, if they exist?
Many biological functions involving simple physical mechanisms 
(flying, sonar, etc.) have proved reasonably straightforward 
to implement.  A function involving quantum coherence between proteins 
or protein catalyzed structures should be quite easy to manipulate, by 
modifying the protein sequences involved via genetic engineering.  
Following arguments given by Drexler et. al. about the limitations of 
DNA coding and evolution, natural quantum coherence should also be easy 
to improve upon.  If those arguing for quantum coherence are serious 
about it, rather than just grinding another axe against AI, they should 
be taking out patents right about now.  

